WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.057
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4621.

2
00:00:01.057 --> 00:00:02.114
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.114 --> 00:00:05.286
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>How does the sound . . . How does it sound, Beck?

4
00:00:05.286 --> 00:00:05.638
Cool.

5
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:05.990
Alright.

6
00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:07.400
And we are recording.

7
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.162
Today is May eleventh, 2016.

8
00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:13.038
We are at Ms. Charlotte Bernard’s home in Moss Bluff [Louisiana].

9
00:00:13.038 --> 00:00:18.324
I’m Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU Libraries.

10
00:00:18.324 --> 00:00:23.610
And I’m here today with Ms. Charlotte Bernard to talk about her memories of Mossville,

11
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:30.657
Louisiana as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

12
00:00:30.657 --> 00:00:35.238
Thank you so much for your time and inviting us into your home.

13
00:00:35.238 --> 00:00:37.000
We really appreciate it.

14
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:37.412
<v CHARLOTTE BERNARD>Alright.

15
00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:46.059
<v DRAGOON>I’m just going to start with a few basic questions, and also should know this is our first interview session together.

16
00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:46.471
<v BERNARD>Okay.

17
00:00:46.471 --> 00:00:51.000
<v DRAGOON>Can you please state your full name for the recording?

18
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.333
<v BERNARD>My name is Charlotte Bernard.

19
00:00:54.333 --> 00:01:09.000
I live in Moss Bluff, and I live with my two parents, Dorothy Bernard and John Bernard, and my granddaughter Alyssa Meyer.

20
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
<v DRAGOON>When and where were you born?

21
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.667
<v BERNARD>I was born in Houston, Texas, 1954.

22
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:23.000
<v DRAGOON>And when did you move to Mossville?

23
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
<v BERNARD>I moved to Mossville when I was seven years old.

24
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:41.000
My dad was living in Houston and he got a job in the plants, Conoco at that time, and we just moved to Mossville near Conoco.

25
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
<v DRAGOON>And what was your first impression of Mossville?

26
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
<v BERNARD>My first impression . . . Excitement because they always had something going on over there that . . . was interesting.

27
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.500
I loved it.

28
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.000
I loved it very much.

29
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.500
They had the swimming pool across the street where we went every day.

30
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.500
And then they had the ballpark.

31
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:12.500
When we got out of the pool we went to the ballpark.

32
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.000
So I really had a good childhood living over there.

33
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.190
<v DRAGOON>What part of Mossville did you live in?

34
00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.810
<v BERNARD>By the Rigmaiden Recreation Center.

35
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.905
Right across the street.

36
00:02:26.905 --> 00:02:33.190
Right directly across the street from the recreation swimming pool and ballpark.

37
00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.333
So I lived in best areas.

38
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:39.476
<v DRAGOON>What did your house look like?

39
00:02:39.476 --> 00:02:44.714
<v BERNARD>My house . . . I loved my house because it was big.

40
00:02:44.714 --> 00:02:54.667
And my daddy and mama raised a lot of . . . I had a lot of brothers and sisters, and me.

41
00:02:54.667 --> 00:03:02.000
All played together and got along good together and swim and played together.

42
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.429
<v DRAGOON>How many siblings did you have?

43
00:03:05.429 --> 00:03:08.286
<v BERNARD>Six sisters and three brothers.

44
00:03:08.286 --> 00:03:10.000
Six including myself.

45
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:10.571
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

46
00:03:10.571 --> 00:03:18.000
What were some of your favorite activities when you were growing up?

47
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.219
<v BERNARD>My favorite . . . Well I loved going to school.

48
00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:24.328
I love eating [laughs].

49
00:03:24.328 --> 00:03:28.020
And playing with my brothers and sisters.

50
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:46.478
Walking the brick wall and my daddy didn’t like us to walk around the brick wall because it was . . . his houses had brick and it was just big enough for us to walk around it.

51
00:03:46.478 --> 00:03:50.169
So we were doing it for fun.

52
00:03:50.169 --> 00:03:53.861
<v DRAGOON>And did your mother work as well?

53
00:03:53.861 --> 00:04:03.353
<v BERNARD>No, she would go to a church, Saint John Bosco in Westlake [Louisiana] and she was a teacher.

54
00:04:03.353 --> 00:04:13.373
She would have classes and she would go pick up the kids and she would bring them to church.

55
00:04:13.373 --> 00:04:16.537
<v DRAGOON>Did your grandparents live in Mossville?

56
00:04:16.537 --> 00:04:34.468
<v BERNARD>No, they . . . My dad had a van and we would go over there just about every other week or every week and on weekends and go to Lafayette [Louisiana] and they call that Duson.

57
00:04:34.468 --> 00:04:37.104
We would go in Duson.

58
00:04:37.104 --> 00:04:40.269
We would stay awhile in Duson.

59
00:04:40.269 --> 00:04:48.706
Then we go to my mom’s people and she lived . . . They lived in Lafayette around Evangeline.

60
00:04:48.706 --> 00:04:51.343
Yeah they was real nice.

61
00:04:51.343 --> 00:04:53.453
I enjoyed my family.

62
00:04:53.453 --> 00:04:59.781
We did a lot of fun things and went with our parents.

63
00:04:59.781 --> 00:05:04.000
They showed us a lot of attention.

64
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.746
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of things did you do as a family together?

65
00:05:08.746 --> 00:05:10.472
<v BERNARD>Right now, or then?

66
00:05:10.472 --> 00:05:10.904
<v DRAGOON>Then.

67
00:05:10.904 --> 00:05:20.396
<v BERNARD>Oh, then we’d get my family . . . When we had a hard time that’s when . . . But we never knew we had hard time,

68
00:05:20.396 --> 00:05:24.279
but my daddy and them would have hard time.

69
00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:31.614
We would have . . . they say we would have them egg . . . There wouldn’t be the real chicken egg.

70
00:05:31.614 --> 00:05:37.223
There would be the other kind of egg like [potter?] egg or something.

71
00:05:37.223 --> 00:05:46.284
My mama would fix that up and she would make some bread and we went swimming and we came back home.

72
00:05:46.284 --> 00:05:49.305
We couldn’t wait to get back home.

73
00:05:49.305 --> 00:05:54.482
She had them bread rising in the oven and she had squash.

74
00:05:54.482 --> 00:05:58.365
And we just enjoyed living and doing things together.

75
00:05:58.365 --> 00:06:02.249
My dad would comb our hair in the morning.

76
00:06:02.249 --> 00:06:04.406
We would go to school.

77
00:06:04.406 --> 00:06:06.995
My mom would make our dresses.

78
00:06:06.995 --> 00:06:14.761
And we would go to school with our little ribbons in our hair and we really enjoyed that.

79
00:06:14.761 --> 00:06:19.508
Enjoyed being together, enjoyed our friends, everybody loved us at school.

80
00:06:19.508 --> 00:06:25.548
We was in talent shows, and modeling, and different things and I really loved

81
00:06:25.548 --> 00:06:25.980
<c.p>it.

82
00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.000
<v DRAGOON>Where did you go to school?

83
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.374
<v BERNARD>I went in my younger . . . My childhood I went to Mossville High.

84
00:06:34.374 --> 00:06:39.747
Then when they integrated they separate us and I went to Westlake.

85
00:06:39.747 --> 00:06:48.255
Some of my good friends went to Sulphur, but a lot of them went to Westlake High with me.

86
00:06:48.255 --> 00:06:50.942
We rode the bus to Westlake.

87
00:06:50.942 --> 00:06:52.734
<v DRAGOON>What was that like?

88
00:06:52.734 --> 00:07:02.585
<v BERNARD>I had to get used of it, being with different race because the school I went to was Mossville was all black.

89
00:07:02.585 --> 00:07:11.989
Then I went to this white and black . . . where they had a lot of whites and had a lot of blacks.

90
00:07:11.989 --> 00:07:15.571
And I had to get used to that,

91
00:07:15.571 --> 00:07:24.080
so I got used to that and made a lot of friends and it was good just like Mossville.

92
00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.214
I made the best out of everything.

93
00:07:27.214 --> 00:07:36.170
That’s what . . . and I was always the one that’s playing with my sisters and brothers trying to mend up everything.

94
00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:42.887
When they would get in trouble I would try to fix it up for them.

95
00:07:42.887 --> 00:07:44.679
Try to help people.

96
00:07:44.679 --> 00:07:47.365
I would . . . I did my best.

97
00:07:47.365 --> 00:07:54.978
Then when I was about to finish school I went to Moss . . . I mean to Westlake High.

98
00:07:54.978 --> 00:07:59.904
And that’s when me and my sisters, we all got together.

99
00:07:59.904 --> 00:08:07.964
My dad had a pasture across the street by the ballpark and I said, well, you know what?

100
00:08:07.964 --> 00:08:10.651
I heard Mama and Dad talking.

101
00:08:10.651 --> 00:08:16.473
And they say . . . My daddy saying, “Honey we got a cow that’s sick.

102
00:08:16.473 --> 00:08:23.637
I’m going to go sell that,” I mean, “Kill that cow because the cow is sick.”

103
00:08:23.637 --> 00:08:27.668
And I heard that and I said, “Uh uh.”

104
00:08:27.668 --> 00:08:29.907
Trying to solve that problem.

105
00:08:29.907 --> 00:08:36.176
I’m going to make that cow well and we’re going to split the money.

106
00:08:36.176 --> 00:08:42.893
So I went across the street . . . No, before I went across the street I told

107
00:08:42.893 --> 00:08:53.192
<c.p>my sisters, I said we’d all get together and go to the store and buy some different things to give to the cow.

108
00:08:53.192 --> 00:08:55.879
So we went to the store.

109
00:08:55.879 --> 00:08:58.118
And the lady knew us.

110
00:08:58.118 --> 00:09:03.940
She was just laughing at us because we was just giggling and everything.

111
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:12.000
And I got some Dewees, some Pepto Bismol and different kind of medicine to give the cow.

112
00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:19.587
And I was the leader and I was telling one of my sisters, I say, “Gwen,

113
00:09:19.587 --> 00:09:25.752
you hold the . . . You put the straw in the cow mouth, the hay.”

114
00:09:25.752 --> 00:09:29.545
And I said, “Katie, you hold the hose.”

115
00:09:29.545 --> 00:09:36.658
And I say . . . and then I forgot what I was going to do with them,

116
00:09:36.658 --> 00:09:40.926
but it was something to do with the mouth.

117
00:09:40.926 --> 00:09:46.143
Oh, I was going to put the medicine in the mouth.

118
00:09:46.143 --> 00:09:51.359
So we went across the street and we did all that.

119
00:09:51.359 --> 00:09:54.678
We put the medicine in his mouth.

120
00:09:54.678 --> 00:09:59.420
So a mosquito came and bit me on my arm.

121
00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.791
And I felt the mosquito,

122
00:10:01.791 --> 00:10:15.069
but at that time I was working at an elderly man house because I was in the . . . Either finishing the eleventh or about to finish the eleventh grade,

123
00:10:15.069 --> 00:10:16.491
or the twelfth.

124
00:10:16.491 --> 00:10:24.079
And I was working at this elderly man house and I was feeling myself getting sleepy.

125
00:10:24.079 --> 00:10:28.821
So I say, I wonder what’s that I’m getting sleepy.

126
00:10:28.821 --> 00:10:34.511
So the next day the same thing, I was getting sleepy again.

127
00:10:34.511 --> 00:10:37.356
So I said . . . My mom said,

128
00:10:37.356 --> 00:10:49.686
“Charlotte if you’re getting sleepy like that you better go to the doctor because it’s not normal to just keep going to sleep all the time.”

129
00:10:49.686 --> 00:10:57.747
And at this time they had on the news be careful because of encephalitis coming this way.

130
00:10:57.747 --> 00:11:02.963
And they said to protect yourself with sprays for the mosquito.

131
00:11:02.963 --> 00:11:04.860
And I started thinking.

132
00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.231
I said, oh my God.

133
00:11:07.231 --> 00:11:14.344
I say, I got bitten by a mosquito and I didn’t pay it no mind.

134
00:11:14.344 --> 00:11:17.189
And I just kept getting sleepy.

135
00:11:17.189 --> 00:11:26.673
So I was at that elderly man house and I was cooking for him on the stove and I said,

136
00:11:26.673 --> 00:11:29.993
“Mr. Delafosse,” I said, “Come eat, baby.”

137
00:11:29.993 --> 00:11:33.786
And he . . . I fixed his food and stuff.

138
00:11:33.786 --> 00:11:42.796
And he ate and he spit it back on the plate and he said, “Charlotte, that was too salty.”

139
00:11:42.796 --> 00:11:49.909
He said, “You must have poured a lot, a lot of salt on that food!”

140
00:11:49.909 --> 00:11:53.229
And I say, “Yeah I’m sorry, sir.

141
00:11:53.229 --> 00:11:55.600
I’m sorry I didn’t mean

142
00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:56.074
<c.p>that.”

143
00:11:56.074 --> 00:11:58.445
And he said, “What’s wrong?

144
00:11:58.445 --> 00:12:00.342
You don’t look good.”

145
00:12:00.342 --> 00:12:04.135
And I said, “I don’t . . . I’m just sleepy.

146
00:12:04.135 --> 00:12:07.929
I want to just sleep all the time.”

147
00:12:07.929 --> 00:12:12.197
He said, “Well you know what I can do?”

148
00:12:12.197 --> 00:12:14.093
I say, “What sir?”

149
00:12:14.093 --> 00:12:22.629
He say, “Go lay down in that bed and I’m going wake you up when your dad come.”

150
00:12:22.629 --> 00:12:25.000
So I did that.

151
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:31.960
And when I went back home I was sleeping in the bed with my husband.

152
00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:37.993
I didn’t want to spend the night to my mama and my daddy.

153
00:12:37.993 --> 00:12:45.417
I had done gotten married because I had married when I was in the twelfth grade.

154
00:12:45.417 --> 00:12:53.769
And I was sleeping [with my dad?] and I had got up to make my husband some coffee.

155
00:12:53.769 --> 00:12:56.089
I don’t know about this.

156
00:12:56.089 --> 00:13:00.730
My brother had told me that that’s what I did.

157
00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:04.442
I got up to make him some coffee.

158
00:13:04.442 --> 00:13:11.402
And when I got up I start falling and foaming at the mouth and stuff.

159
00:13:11.402 --> 00:13:16.506
And they called the ambulance and nobody knew what I had.

160
00:13:16.506 --> 00:13:21.610
I went to several hospitals and nobody knew what I had.

161
00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:35.067
But they said it looked . . . They brought me to one in New Orleans to the hospital and they said it sound . . . its symptoms sound like it could be encephalitis.

162
00:13:35.067 --> 00:13:47.132
So and then they say we don’t know what really it is, but we say that it might be encephalitis, so that’s what I went with.

163
00:13:47.132 --> 00:13:48.060
Being encephalitis.

164
00:13:48.060 --> 00:14:03.836
But I was . . . really it was like night into day at one time because I was very confused because I was a young teenager and I had . . . was riding horses and just enjoying life.

165
00:14:03.836 --> 00:14:17.757
Swimming and doing different events and stuff and just starting to go out and date and stuff and when I got sick they brought me home and I had tubes.

166
00:14:17.757 --> 00:14:22.861
I had to wear Pampers and I couldn’t talk or anything.

167
00:14:22.861 --> 00:14:26.573
And so it brought me down a lot.

168
00:14:26.573 --> 00:14:38.174
And they told me that if . . . not to show me a mirror because if I see in a mirror then I’ll see how I look.

169
00:14:38.174 --> 00:14:48.846
My brother . . . my mama had left . . . my mama and my daddy had left and went to the store and they told my brother,

170
00:14:48.846 --> 00:14:52.094
he said, “Don’t give Charlotte a mirror.”

171
00:14:52.094 --> 00:14:57.199
So we would play a game and he said, “You ugly?”

172
00:14:57.199 --> 00:14:59.983
And I would shake my head.

173
00:14:59.983 --> 00:15:02.767
And he said, “Are you pretty?”

174
00:15:02.767 --> 00:15:04.159
<c.p>And I was.

175
00:15:04.159 --> 00:15:15.759
And then after that . . . And I made a noise for him to come to give me a book and I was pointing to a book.

176
00:15:15.759 --> 00:15:26.896
Then he gave me a book, they had a picture of a mirror and he say, “You want that mirror?” and I say, “Yeah.”

177
00:15:26.896 --> 00:15:32.000
I didn’t say yeah, but I didn’t shook my head.

178
00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:48.204
And he gave me the mirror and I looked in that mirror and I seen myself in that mirror and I looked like a little creature because I had bumps all over my face.

179
00:15:48.204 --> 00:15:51.063
My jaw was out of place.

180
00:15:51.063 --> 00:15:54.876
It was about two inches from the bottom.

181
00:15:54.876 --> 00:15:57.259
And I had no hair.

182
00:15:57.259 --> 00:15:57.735
Ooh!

183
00:15:57.735 --> 00:16:09.650
And I seen that I just started crying and freaking out and stuff and my brother was scared because my daddy had done told him,

184
00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.033
“Don’t give her no mirror.”

185
00:16:12.033 --> 00:16:18.705
And just from then on I just . . . I wanted to give up in life.

186
00:16:18.705 --> 00:16:24.900
That was gone, Mossville, and I didn’t see myself having fun no more.

187
00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:29.666
My sister and them was going out and having fun.

188
00:16:29.666 --> 00:16:32.049
I was in the wheelchair.

189
00:16:32.049 --> 00:16:38.244
But I say well life goes on and I just kept it up.

190
00:16:38.244 --> 00:16:42.534
And I was married and I had a son.

191
00:16:42.534 --> 00:16:47.299
He was . . . I think my baby was four years old.

192
00:16:47.299 --> 00:16:53.495
And my mama was help raising him and stuff since I was sick.

193
00:16:53.495 --> 00:17:00.644
And I just say, well I just kept saying I’m not going to get better.

194
00:17:00.644 --> 00:17:05.886
Then I say, no I’m not going to think like that.

195
00:17:05.886 --> 00:17:15.418
Then I start giving my own self therapy and then gone from the tubes and God’s been good to me.

196
00:17:15.418 --> 00:17:20.183
And I got off the tubes and I started walking.

197
00:17:20.183 --> 00:17:23.996
Practicing walking on the back of my wheelchair.

198
00:17:23.996 --> 00:17:38.293
And I started back walking and I . . . They brought me to therapy in Lake Charles [Louisiana] and they gave me a Hemi walker that walks when somebody have one side.

199
00:17:38.293 --> 00:17:47.348
Well I couldn’t use my right side, but I could use my left and I would walk with that.

200
00:17:47.348 --> 00:17:50.208
And I was doing real good.

201
00:17:50.208 --> 00:17:52.591
I was proud of myself.

202
00:17:52.591 --> 00:18:01.169
And I had two other kids besides that one and I raised both of my children by myself.

203
00:18:01.169 --> 00:18:08.794
And I thank God for that because right now they all on their own doing wonderful.

204
00:18:08.794 --> 00:18:23.092
And I always did have myself . . . Say to myself when my children get old enough I don’t want them begging for my money and wanting cigarettes or nothing from me.

205
00:18:23.092 --> 00:18:24.521
I want them

206
00:18:24.521 --> 00:18:27.857
<c.p>to be independent and on their own.

207
00:18:27.857 --> 00:18:32.147
So when they did that that really pleased me.

208
00:18:32.147 --> 00:18:36.912
So I’ve been living by myself since two years ago.

209
00:18:36.912 --> 00:18:42.631
I would help . . . My mama have dementia, Alzheimer’s, and she’s a diabetic.

210
00:18:42.631 --> 00:18:52.163
I’ve been leaving my house, getting on my scooter and driving to her house . . . about twenty yards from my house.

211
00:18:52.163 --> 00:19:02.648
I would get on my scooter, drive my scooter to her house and stay there all day and take care of her.

212
00:19:02.648 --> 00:19:04.077
Help bathe her.

213
00:19:04.077 --> 00:19:11.226
She would get in the bathtub and I could get around good with my walker.

214
00:19:11.226 --> 00:19:16.468
I just needed something to hold onto or just be careful.

215
00:19:16.468 --> 00:19:20.758
And I would help her and I would cook.

216
00:19:20.758 --> 00:19:26.000
I’d see about the mail and I answer the phone.

217
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.199
I was a pest to my daddy.

218
00:19:29.199 --> 00:19:38.339
My daddy always said that I was not doing something right, but he would pick on me all the time.

219
00:19:38.339 --> 00:19:40.624
And I started getting weak.

220
00:19:40.624 --> 00:19:48.851
When I would go home I would fall down and my daddy had to come pick me up.

221
00:19:48.851 --> 00:19:58.905
And then he did it so often where he told me, he say, “Charlotte, I want you to come live with us.”

222
00:19:58.905 --> 00:20:08.045
He said that I’ll pick you up and then you can go back when you stop and that falling time.

223
00:20:08.045 --> 00:20:08.959
Always falling.

224
00:20:08.959 --> 00:20:11.701
Then you can go back home.

225
00:20:11.701 --> 00:20:13.986
So that’s what I did.

226
00:20:13.986 --> 00:20:24.498
And I’m here today and I thank God that I’m here because I have two wonderful parents that do a lot for me.

227
00:20:24.498 --> 00:20:41.407
I don’t want to go with my children because I know married people how they argue and want to do . . . they probably saying doing things and I figure I can help my mama more than they think.

228
00:20:41.407 --> 00:20:47.348
So I’m over here doing things for them and trying to get around.

229
00:20:47.348 --> 00:20:50.090
And I . . . but I loved Mossville.

230
00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:54.204
And when I left . . . it was kind of sad,

231
00:20:54.204 --> 00:21:02.430
but I say well we’re going to go on and live somewhere else and start a new beginning.

232
00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:07.000
So we came over here and I love it.

233
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.500
<v DRAGOON>When did you leave Mossville?

234
00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:10.000
<v BERNARD>Huh?

235
00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.000
<v DRAGOON>When did you leave Mossville?

236
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:19.222
<v BERNARD>About a year and a half . . . about a year . . . No,

237
00:21:19.222 --> 00:21:27.933
about a year and a few months because we left in February last year.

238
00:21:27.933 --> 00:21:32.911
So that’s how long we’ve been over here.

239
00:21:32.911 --> 00:21:41.000
<v DRAGOON>What are some of your favorite memories from growing up in Mossville?

240
00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.309
<v BERNARD>Just playing with everybody in Mossville.

241
00:21:44.309 --> 00:21:47.619
We was like a big family.

242
00:21:47.619 --> 00:21:51.480
And we all got together and played.

243
00:21:51.480 --> 00:22:03.063
We had different activities, swimming pool, ballpark, and just playing with the children and helping Mom do things around the house.

244
00:22:03.063 --> 00:22:06.372
And working and coming back home.

245
00:22:06.372 --> 00:22:09.682
We just had a nice time.

246
00:22:09.682 --> 00:22:17.404
But it kind of made me sad because some people was dying in Mossville.

247
00:22:17.404 --> 00:22:25.126
That was kind of sad because we didn’t know what they was dying from.

248
00:22:25.126 --> 00:22:33.951
And always did say it might, it could have been the water that we were drinking.

249
00:22:33.951 --> 00:22:37.812
I didn’t really know what it was.

250
00:22:37.812 --> 00:22:48.843
But I only stuck around for . . . I moved to Lake Charles for a long time after I had got married.

251
00:22:48.843 --> 00:22:51.601
And got . . . I was disabled.

252
00:22:51.601 --> 00:23:04.287
I moved to Lake Charles and I say I wanted to be on my own and do things by myself so I moved.

253
00:23:04.287 --> 00:23:14.215
But after that my children, when they got grown enough, they said “Mom, let’s go back to Mossville.

254
00:23:14.215 --> 00:23:16.973
We want to see Mossville.”

255
00:23:16.973 --> 00:23:23.592
Then I would tell them about the memories we had in Mossville.

256
00:23:23.592 --> 00:23:30.211
So he wanted to get some of these things that I did.

257
00:23:30.211 --> 00:23:33.520
The fun that I had made.

258
00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:40.139
He wanted to have the fun so we moved back to Mossville.

259
00:23:40.139 --> 00:23:44.000
<v DRAGOON>Did your mother work growing up?

260
00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:44.590
<v BERNARD>[Disagrees].

261
00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:47.541
I don’t remember her working.

262
00:23:47.541 --> 00:23:58.754
The only job she did I told you earlier, she was a Sunday school teacher at Saint John Bosco.

263
00:23:58.754 --> 00:24:09.377
And she would go pick up the kids . . . Some of the kids and bring them to Sunday school.

264
00:24:09.377 --> 00:24:14.098
<v DRAGOON>How has the community changed in your lifetime?

265
00:24:14.098 --> 00:24:15.869
The Mossville community.

266
00:24:15.869 --> 00:24:20.000
How have you seen it change?

267
00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:27.164
<v BERNARD>Well to me, when I was a young girl or right now?

268
00:24:27.164 --> 00:24:29.552
<v DRAGOON>How has . . . What’s different?

269
00:24:29.552 --> 00:24:34.925
How have you seen it change throughout your lifetime?

270
00:24:34.925 --> 00:24:45.075
<v BERNARD>Well it was . . . Mossville was always Mossville, but it changed when they started to buy out Mossville.

271
00:24:45.075 --> 00:25:00.000
Sasol bought out Mossville and they started having people leaving and they started knocking down trees, and Mossville didn’t look like Mossville to us anymore.

272
00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.179
And it looked like a ghost town.

273
00:25:04.179 --> 00:25:04.776
Yeah.

274
00:25:04.776 --> 00:25:16.119
And my son that I had, he lived in Mossville, but then he got married and went to Houston.

275
00:25:16.119 --> 00:25:23.284
And he would tell me, “Mom, I want to come back home.”

276
00:25:23.284 --> 00:25:26.269
I say, “Where is home?”

277
00:25:26.269 --> 00:25:32.239
I was living in Moss Bluff where I am now.

278
00:25:32.239 --> 00:25:36.418
I say, “You want to come here?

279
00:25:36.418 --> 00:25:40.000
You want to go to Mossville?

280
00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:43.582
Where do you want to go?”

281
00:25:43.582 --> 00:25:48.955
And he say, “Oh yeah they is selling Mossville.”

282
00:25:48.955 --> 00:25:56.119
So it’s really . . . it’s a sad thing but it’s a good thing.

283
00:25:56.119 --> 00:25:57.910
It’s a blessing.

284
00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:02.687
It’s a blessing for a lot of people.

285
00:26:02.687 --> 00:26:10.448
A lot of the elderly people can’t handle the idea of them moving

286
00:26:10.448 --> 00:26:14.627
<c.p>because they live their life in Mossville.

287
00:26:14.627 --> 00:26:22.388
And a lot of people died . . . they still die, sometimes two and three.

288
00:26:22.388 --> 00:26:25.373
They just dying in Mossville.

289
00:26:25.373 --> 00:26:31.343
I guess they . . . That’s where they want to die at.

290
00:26:31.343 --> 00:26:32.537
In Mossville.

291
00:26:32.537 --> 00:26:35.522
They don’t want to leave.

292
00:26:35.522 --> 00:26:37.313
That’s they area.

293
00:26:37.313 --> 00:26:41.493
But me, I’m a senior citizen now.

294
00:26:41.493 --> 00:26:47.463
I’m sixty-one years old and I love it over here.

295
00:26:47.463 --> 00:26:49.254
It’s a blessing.

296
00:26:49.254 --> 00:26:52.239
And my dad love it.

297
00:26:52.239 --> 00:26:55.224
My mom love it, too.

298
00:26:55.224 --> 00:27:00.000
<v DRAGOON>Did you have a garden growing up?

299
00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.134
<v BERNARD>A garden . . . My daddy did.

300
00:27:02.134 --> 00:27:06.402
And I would go watch him tend to the garden.

301
00:27:06.402 --> 00:27:13.232
He had a garden at someone else’s house and he would go check on the garden.

302
00:27:13.232 --> 00:27:19.207
So I loved that idea of walking with him, and discussing things, and laughing.

303
00:27:19.207 --> 00:27:26.463
And walking to the garden and picking things out the garden and bringing them in the house.

304
00:27:26.463 --> 00:27:27.744
I liked that.

305
00:27:27.744 --> 00:27:30.732
<v DRAGOON>And you said you had a cow.

306
00:27:30.732 --> 00:27:35.000
Did you have a lot of other animals, too?

307
00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:41.583
<v BERNARD>Well I remember two or three cows, and I think it was two horses.

308
00:27:41.583 --> 00:27:46.285
And I just see myself with . . . They had a chicken.

309
00:27:46.285 --> 00:27:51.928
I remember they had the goose, but the goose wasn’t for us.

310
00:27:51.928 --> 00:27:54.749
But the chicken was for us.

311
00:27:54.749 --> 00:28:06.975
And the chicken would run and try to peck me and I had to hurry up in the house before the rooster not to peck me.

312
00:28:06.975 --> 00:28:14.969
But . . . And then I rode the horse, and we went and helped my daddy milk the cow.

313
00:28:14.969 --> 00:28:21.082
But the funniest thing and most embarrassing stuff, when . . . Okay, they had ballgames.

314
00:28:21.082 --> 00:28:28.135
And my daddy would go and milk the cow at five o’clock in the afternoon.

315
00:28:28.135 --> 00:28:30.956
That’s when they started playing ball.

316
00:28:30.956 --> 00:28:37.539
So they were playing ball and my daddy would say, “Well honey, I’m going

317
00:28:37.539 --> 00:28:39.420
<c.p>to milk the cows.”

318
00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:43.182
My daddy would go and milk the cow.

319
00:28:43.182 --> 00:28:49.295
When he get across the street they had people playing ball and stuff.

320
00:28:49.295 --> 00:28:55.878
He would cut through the field right where they were playing ball at! [Laughs].

321
00:28:55.878 --> 00:28:57.759
That was his routine.

322
00:28:57.759 --> 00:29:01.520
That’s what he did, went milked the cow.

323
00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:06.693
And he would come back with his little bucket with milk.

324
00:29:06.693 --> 00:29:16.567
And they had to stop the game and just let him pass through the thing and go home with the milk.

325
00:29:16.567 --> 00:29:19.389
So I really enjoyed my childhood.

326
00:29:19.389 --> 00:29:21.270
Had a wonderful childhood.

327
00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:25.031
Owe it to my mom and my dad.

328
00:29:25.031 --> 00:29:33.025
We always bumping heads together because he always saying, “Well, Charlotte, if you don’t [learn?],” he say,

329
00:29:33.025 --> 00:29:41.489
“I’m going to send you on this road and you’re going to go to your son or whatever.”

330
00:29:41.489 --> 00:29:44.310
I say, “I ain’t going nowhere.

331
00:29:44.310 --> 00:29:48.072
I’m sticking to you like crazy glue.” [Laughs].

332
00:29:48.072 --> 00:29:55.596
Because I had a wonderful childhood and I want to stay here and enjoy my parents.

333
00:29:55.596 --> 00:30:04.060
I might be gone before them but I want to spend the rest of my life with them.

334
00:30:04.060 --> 00:30:05.000
Yeah.

335
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:09.500
<v DRAGOON>How . . . you said you were . . . How old were you when you got married?

336
00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:11.577
<v BERNARD>I was in the twelfth grade.

337
00:30:11.577 --> 00:30:14.000
<v DRAGOON>You were in the twelfth grade?

338
00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:15.826
<v BERNARD>Before I graduated.

339
00:30:15.826 --> 00:30:16.435
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

340
00:30:16.435 --> 00:30:17.043
<v BERNARD>[Agrees].

341
00:30:17.043 --> 00:30:20.696
<v DRAGOON>And what was your husband’s name?

342
00:30:20.696 --> 00:30:22.522
<v BERNARD>Daryl Joseph Walker.

343
00:30:22.522 --> 00:30:23.130
Yeah.

344
00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:28.000
<v DRAGOON>And you had . . . You have two children?

345
00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:31.069
<v BERNARD>I have one before I got sick.

346
00:30:31.069 --> 00:30:33.700
It was Anthony, my oldest one.

347
00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:38.084
And I had two more after I got sick, so.

348
00:30:38.084 --> 00:30:42.906
But the doctors told me I couldn’t have any more kids.

349
00:30:42.906 --> 00:30:48.167
But he told me I wouldn’t walk again and I walked, so.

350
00:30:48.167 --> 00:30:53.867
He’s not God and they don’t know what I can do or couldn’t.

351
00:30:53.867 --> 00:30:56.936
At that time I just did everything.

352
00:30:56.936 --> 00:31:01.320
I didn’t pay no mind to what I couldn’t do.

353
00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:04.828
I wanted to prove what I could do.

354
00:31:04.828 --> 00:31:06.581
And I did that.

355
00:31:06.581 --> 00:31:09.650
I can pat myself of the back.

356
00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:13.158
I did a good job with my kids.

357
00:31:13.158 --> 00:31:14.911
I have one boy.

358
00:31:14.911 --> 00:31:17.542
My oldest boy’s a hot head.

359
00:31:17.542 --> 00:31:24.118
And well he . . . His temper flares up a lot, but he’s alive and everybody different.

360
00:31:24.118 --> 00:31:26.310
So I deals with him.

361
00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:40.340
They all have . . . Everybody got problems and I just deal with them individual because even though they married and gone I still have to get on that phone and talk to them,

362
00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:46.039
and preach to them, and do . . . I try to be a good mom.

363
00:31:46.039 --> 00:31:53.931
And they said they’re very proud of me so I guess I got to go with that! [Laughs].

364
00:31:53.931 --> 00:31:54.369
Yeah.

365
00:31:54.369 --> 00:31:57.000
<v DRAGOON>How old are they now?

366
00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:05.250
<v BERNARD>My oldest son is forty . . . Well I’ll say like this.

367
00:32:05.250 --> 00:32:07.725
He’s twenty years . . .

368
00:32:07.725 --> 00:32:10.200
<c.p>let me see.

369
00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:13.500
Anthony is about forty-something.

370
00:32:13.500 --> 00:32:14.325
Forty-seven.

371
00:32:14.325 --> 00:32:22.575
<v DRAGOON>And what did you do with your family growing up?

372
00:32:22.575 --> 00:32:30.000
What kinds of activities did you do together?

373
00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.845
<v BERNARD>Hang around my family.

374
00:32:31.845 --> 00:32:40.611
And what was the funny part, my dad, he had a lot of girls, and was six with me.

375
00:32:40.611 --> 00:32:48.915
He would sit on the porch with a gun and he would never do nothing with that gun,

376
00:32:48.915 --> 00:32:53.067
but he would sit on it with the gun.

377
00:32:53.067 --> 00:32:59.065
And everybody pass and that’s what they starting called him, “Hey, Uncle John!”

378
00:32:59.065 --> 00:33:06.446
They wanted that uncle to be in there because they knew my daddy was something else.

379
00:33:06.446 --> 00:33:08.753
He would scare the kids.

380
00:33:08.753 --> 00:33:14.289
Like if we stayed to the dance he’ll come with his gun,

381
00:33:14.289 --> 00:33:21.671
and he’ll come get us and say, “Didn’t I tell y’all it’s time to come home?

382
00:33:21.671 --> 00:33:23.978
Well you better start walking.”

383
00:33:23.978 --> 00:33:27.668
Then had a little trail of us kids.

384
00:33:27.668 --> 00:33:29.514
About four of them.

385
00:33:29.514 --> 00:33:33.204
We would all start walking going back home.

386
00:33:33.204 --> 00:33:38.279
And boy them guys would just be looking, “Bye, Uncle John!”

387
00:33:38.279 --> 00:33:40.125
So we had fun.

388
00:33:40.125 --> 00:33:42.431
We really enjoyed our self.

389
00:33:42.431 --> 00:33:47.506
But right now I’m over here and I never get sick.

390
00:33:47.506 --> 00:33:50.736
And I just look at everything positive.

391
00:33:50.736 --> 00:33:55.810
I will bring my own self to the doctor for checkups,

392
00:33:55.810 --> 00:34:03.653
but I won’t take no pills and I owe that to my father because his mom and,

393
00:34:03.653 --> 00:34:10.112
they had Indian in they blood and they didn’t believe in medicine . . . Drug medicine.

394
00:34:10.112 --> 00:34:18.878
And I didn’t believe in pills so I didn’t really get sick and had to go to the doctor.

395
00:34:18.878 --> 00:34:24.875
So my sister . . . before I moved my sister told me, she say, “Charlotte,

396
00:34:24.875 --> 00:34:33.641
write down some of the things that’s wrong with you that you want to go to the doctor for.”

397
00:34:33.641 --> 00:34:46.097
So I wrote down a few and that’s what I’m doing right now because once you go to the doctor they always find something wrong with you.

398
00:34:46.097 --> 00:34:54.401
And I’d say I wish I wouldn’t have started going, but I think it’s doing me some good.

399
00:34:54.401 --> 00:34:54.863
I

400
00:34:54.863 --> 00:34:58.092
<c.p>look at it in a positive way.

401
00:34:58.092 --> 00:35:03.167
I’m doing an overhaul just like they do an old car.

402
00:35:03.167 --> 00:35:08.703
They want to fix it up and bring it back to life.

403
00:35:08.703 --> 00:35:11.933
Well that’s what they doing with me.

404
00:35:11.933 --> 00:35:23.005
And I know that God got me in his plan that I’m . . . That he’s not finished with me yet and I’m doing an overhaul.

405
00:35:23.005 --> 00:35:32.232
And they talking about surgery on my arm because they say I have a . . . Some kind of arthritis [phone ringing].

406
00:35:32.232 --> 00:35:33.155
Hello [brother?].

407
00:35:33.155 --> 00:35:35.000
<v UNKNOWN>What up, Charlotte?

408
00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:36.875
<v BERNARD>Oh, nothing.

409
00:35:36.875 --> 00:35:42.500
I’m having an interview with Mossville.

410
00:35:42.500 --> 00:35:45.312
I’m [. . .?] right here.

411
00:35:45.312 --> 00:35:47.188
<v UNKNOWN>Oh, okay.

412
00:35:47.188 --> 00:35:48.125
<v BERNARD>Daddy.

413
00:35:48.125 --> 00:35:50.000
<v DADDY>[Pardon?]?

414
00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:51.523
<v BERNARD>It’s Tyra.

415
00:35:51.523 --> 00:35:52.284
<v DADDY>Tyra?

416
00:35:52.284 --> 00:35:53.045
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

417
00:35:53.045 --> 00:35:55.329
<v DADDY>I got it.

418
00:35:55.329 --> 00:35:56.090
Hello?

419
00:35:56.090 --> 00:35:56.851
Hey.

420
00:35:56.851 --> 00:35:59.135
<v BERNARD>Sorry about that.

421
00:35:59.135 --> 00:36:01.419
<v DRAGOON>Oh, that’s okay.

422
00:36:01.419 --> 00:36:10.554
I’m just thinking about what question I want to ask you next.

423
00:36:10.554 --> 00:36:16.644
Where did you go to church growing up?

424
00:36:16.644 --> 00:36:26.541
<v BERNARD>I went to church at Saint John Bosco when I was a teenager,

425
00:36:26.541 --> 00:36:39.482
but then when I got a little older I said that it wasn’t really teaching me much.

426
00:36:39.482 --> 00:36:56.991
Then I started going to another church Momma was going to because Momma had left Saint John and went to another church, Pastor [?].

427
00:36:56.991 --> 00:37:06.126
So she was going to Pastor [?] and I was . . . I started going.

428
00:37:06.126 --> 00:37:12.977
I was living, staying with a man Jean Hill.

429
00:37:12.977 --> 00:37:25.158
So I started going to his church where he was, Church of Christ on Mill Street.

430
00:37:25.158 --> 00:37:31.248
And so I went there for a while.

431
00:37:31.248 --> 00:37:47.995
And then after I got in an accident I started going to another church and I didn’t go back to Mill Street.

432
00:37:47.995 --> 00:37:59.414
But right now I’m in a position where I don’t have a ride to church.

433
00:37:59.414 --> 00:38:16.923
And I’m stuck in the middle because my dad be . . . Can’t leave my mama so I don’t be having a ride to church.

434
00:38:16.923 --> 00:38:19.968
I say oh well.

435
00:38:19.968 --> 00:38:24.536
I say God knows what’s happening.

436
00:38:24.536 --> 00:38:32.910
I say one day I’ll get to go to church again.

437
00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:39.000
Right now I’m not going to church.

438
00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:41.694
<v DRAGOON>Was the church really involved with community affairs?

439
00:38:41.694 --> 00:38:49.776
<v BERNARD>No, not the Catholic church because they would only . . . Well in a way they was because my daddy was a Knight of Peter Claver.

440
00:38:49.776 --> 00:38:52.806
That’s what they called . . . A Knight of Peter Claver.

441
00:38:52.806 --> 00:38:56.847
And would see about, give boxes for holidays and stuff like that.

442
00:38:56.847 --> 00:38:58.194
Or if you need

443
00:38:58.194 --> 00:39:01.224
<c.p>help on your utility bill they would help you.

444
00:39:01.224 --> 00:39:05.265
But I went to a church where my mom knew the pastor.

445
00:39:05.265 --> 00:39:08.633
He was raised in Mossville and he became a pastor.

446
00:39:08.633 --> 00:39:09.306
So, yeah.

447
00:39:09.306 --> 00:39:12.000
<v DRAGOON>Who were the community leaders in Mossville?

448
00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:17.847
<v BERNARD>Who were the community leader . . . What you mean by that?

449
00:39:17.847 --> 00:39:26.617
<v DRAGOON>Like were there people that folks looked to if there were issues in the community?

450
00:39:26.617 --> 00:39:30.125
<v BERNARD>We all look at one another.

451
00:39:30.125 --> 00:39:47.665
We was like a big family and we all did things . . . When one did something we listened to what that one person said and that’s how we did our life.

452
00:39:47.665 --> 00:40:08.129
Living . . . Looking at one another and just . . . Mama would make us go to different mom’s house or somebody’s house and clean the house up and see what they needed and we would go do that.

453
00:40:08.129 --> 00:40:12.806
We was raised with the people around Mossville.

454
00:40:12.806 --> 00:40:18.653
So when we got married we still was like that.

455
00:40:18.653 --> 00:40:24.500
We all stuck together and we like that right now.

456
00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:36.194
Right now there are people like one lady we live here and my granddaughter she came to live with us.

457
00:40:36.194 --> 00:40:44.964
And since Christmas of this year she came to stay with us for a while.

458
00:40:44.964 --> 00:40:54.319
She’s going to finish school and she riding on the bus to go to Sam Houston.

459
00:40:54.319 --> 00:40:59.581
And the lady that drive the bus is Phyllis.

460
00:40:59.581 --> 00:41:04.843
And Phyllis is a lady that lived in Mossville.

461
00:41:04.843 --> 00:41:16.536
And so . . . I go to church . . . When I was going to church they have Josh that was on American Idol.

462
00:41:16.536 --> 00:41:19.460
You know Josh that sang?

463
00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:30.569
That had just got from . . . He was picked on American Idol I say about . . . I’d say three years ago.

464
00:41:30.569 --> 00:41:32.323
You don’t remember?

465
00:41:32.323 --> 00:41:37.000
Well that’s the church I went to.

466
00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:37.966
<v DRAGOON>Oh, wow.

467
00:41:37.966 --> 00:41:38.449
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

468
00:41:38.449 --> 00:41:38.932
And . . .

469
00:41:38.932 --> 00:41:39.898
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>Charlotte [. . .?] Josh.

470
00:41:39.898 --> 00:41:40.382
Josh [. . .?]

471
00:41:40.382 --> 00:41:45.212
<v BERNARD>Yeah and Josh came to the church and Josh sang.

472
00:41:45.212 --> 00:41:47.145
And Mama loved Josh.

473
00:41:47.145 --> 00:41:53.425
She would always talk about Josh would go and he would kiss her.

474
00:41:53.425 --> 00:41:57.772
And she respect Josh because he was so sweet.

475
00:41:57.772 --> 00:42:00.671
Yeah, Mama loved her some Josh.

476
00:42:00.671 --> 00:42:14.197
And they have people . . . when I go to the doctor office they have about three or four people that live in Oak Park Boulevard and they from Mossville.

477
00:42:14.197 --> 00:42:19.028
And a lot of people around here are from Mossville.

478
00:42:19.028 --> 00:42:22.409
So we get in touch with everybody.

479
00:42:22.409 --> 00:42:28.689
Then they supposed to have a . . . Like a reunion, I think in July.

480
00:42:28.689 --> 00:42:35.935
So I don’t know if I’m be going to that because of the . . . My disability.

481
00:42:35.935 --> 00:42:37.868
I can’t get around.

482
00:42:37.868 --> 00:42:48.978
And then that don’t give me too much courage to get out there and . . . Because I can’t do the things they be doing.

483
00:42:48.978 --> 00:42:54.292
And it just upsets me and so I’d rather stay home.

484
00:42:54.292 --> 00:43:04.437
And so I might not be going but I’m going to hear about it, or I might see it on Facebook.

485
00:43:04.437 --> 00:43:12.649
You do everything on Facebook I learn . . . because they would . . . my mama have that Alzheimer’s and stuff.

486
00:43:12.649 --> 00:43:20.378
I say and I would hear on TV they saying if you don’t exercise your mind,

487
00:43:20.378 --> 00:43:25.692
more people going to be getting Alzheimer’s and stuff like that.

488
00:43:25.692 --> 00:43:30.523
I say, oh no baby that there is a lie.

489
00:43:30.523 --> 00:43:37.286
I say I’m work on me, I know how to get on the computer.

490
00:43:37.286 --> 00:43:42.600
And I know how to get on the Facebook and stuff.

491
00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:50.812
But after that I bought me a tablet and I started learning how to work that tablet.

492
00:43:50.812 --> 00:43:57.575
And my sister was my caregiver and she would see me cry, and cry.

493
00:43:57.575 --> 00:44:00.957
My sister say, “Girl what you crying

494
00:44:00.957 --> 00:44:01.440
<c.p>for?”

495
00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:04.338
And I say, “I am aggravated!”

496
00:44:04.338 --> 00:44:06.271
And she say, “Aggravated?

497
00:44:06.271 --> 00:44:08.203
What you aggravated for?”

498
00:44:08.203 --> 00:44:10.618
“I can’t work the computer.

499
00:44:10.618 --> 00:44:14.000
I don’t know what to do!”

500
00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:19.830
But . . . after the aggravation . . . after whatever you call it,

501
00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:30.193
well I kept trying, and trying, and trying I finally got on and stayed with Facebook.

502
00:44:30.193 --> 00:44:34.727
I text them, they text me back.

503
00:44:34.727 --> 00:44:37.966
I learned how to text.

504
00:44:37.966 --> 00:44:52.216
Right now I love it because I text my children and give them good advice and yeah, doing real good with it.

505
00:44:52.216 --> 00:44:56.102
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>[When they going to have it?]

506
00:44:56.102 --> 00:44:56.750
<v BERNARD>[Agrees].

507
00:44:56.750 --> 00:44:58.045
In July.

508
00:44:58.045 --> 00:45:11.000
<v DRAGOON>What do you think the major difference was between the education you got in Mossville and your parent’s education?

509
00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:18.307
<v BERNARD>I just see how things have changed from . . . I remember when I was little,

510
00:45:18.307 --> 00:45:23.527
I used to go to a lady named Dorothy Payne.

511
00:45:23.527 --> 00:45:37.098
I used to go to her house and she had a . . . some kind of . . . Well I know now it was a paper and it had blue,

512
00:45:37.098 --> 00:45:38.664
red, yellow, green.

513
00:45:38.664 --> 00:45:46.494
And she would place that on a TV and I would see a color TV.

514
00:45:46.494 --> 00:45:50.147
And I said that was real cool.

515
00:45:50.147 --> 00:45:55.889
And then I seen a real TV and I said, “Wow!”

516
00:45:55.889 --> 00:46:01.109
And then I start seeing different games they would have.

517
00:46:01.109 --> 00:46:13.636
Nintendo and DVDs and all kind of new technology and it just started getting better, and better, and better and I say, “My God!”

518
00:46:13.636 --> 00:46:16.767
I really couldn’t adjust to it.

519
00:46:16.767 --> 00:46:20.421
But I say God is so good.

520
00:46:20.421 --> 00:46:25.641
How things got so advanced from when I was little.

521
00:46:25.641 --> 00:46:31.904
And now we’re sitting down hardly have to go to the movie.

522
00:46:31.904 --> 00:46:36.080
I don’t have to get up and go

523
00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:37.124
<c.p>pay bills.

524
00:46:37.124 --> 00:46:50.173
When I was paying them I would let them go through the bank and they would take out everything in the . . . Right through my checking,

525
00:46:50.173 --> 00:46:51.739
my debit card.

526
00:46:51.739 --> 00:46:59.047
I used to go to the food stamp office and pick up food stamps.

527
00:46:59.047 --> 00:47:07.398
It would have . . . Now you have a card and you just wait until that day come.

528
00:47:07.398 --> 00:47:12.618
And you go to the store and get your card.

529
00:47:12.618 --> 00:47:16.271
So everything done changed; technology done changed.

530
00:47:16.271 --> 00:47:25.667
And it’s good and I love it, but it’s downfall with the kids, the kids are way different.

531
00:47:25.667 --> 00:47:35.062
And they ready to . . . and they don’t have time to go outside and play with all them sisters,

532
00:47:35.062 --> 00:47:39.760
they in the house living on they cell phones.

533
00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:41.326
Like my granddaughter.

534
00:47:41.326 --> 00:47:45.501
She never hardly comes out of the room.

535
00:47:45.501 --> 00:47:56.463
We just had a meeting and then we asked her to try to show her face and get out the house.

536
00:47:56.463 --> 00:48:00.116
Try . . . Come out and talk with us.

537
00:48:00.116 --> 00:48:02.726
And she been doing that.

538
00:48:02.726 --> 00:48:07.424
But kids not kids like they used to be.

539
00:48:07.424 --> 00:48:10.034
They more with the games.

540
00:48:10.034 --> 00:48:16.819
And they minds can’t take the pressure of what people used to do.

541
00:48:16.819 --> 00:48:33.000
They ready to kill they self or do something crazy because they just not ready for life because they always stuck in they room on they computer. [Phone ringing] Sorry!

542
00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:34.211
<v DRAGOON>It’s okay.

543
00:48:34.211 --> 00:48:34.817
<v BERNARD>Hello?

544
00:48:34.817 --> 00:48:37.845
My daddy picked it up.

545
00:48:37.845 --> 00:48:45.719
But my . . . The people around here they see that we’re a big family.

546
00:48:45.719 --> 00:48:55.410
Whenever they have functions all my family come over here and we have a house full.

547
00:48:55.410 --> 00:48:58.438
It look like a parade.

548
00:48:58.438 --> 00:49:11.158
I mean a fair or something because we have a yard full, house full, and we just been enjoying our self.

549
00:49:11.158 --> 00:49:18.426
We have our downfalls and stuff, but I don’t look at downfalls.

550
00:49:18.426 --> 00:49:23.271
I just keep on doing what I’m doing.

551
00:49:23.271 --> 00:49:35.385
I’m a single mom when my husband that got killed and I forgot how much years it was that happened.

552
00:49:35.385 --> 00:49:39.625
But to me it wasn’t long ago.

553
00:49:39.625 --> 00:49:48.104
To me it was just two or three years but it might be longer.

554
00:49:48.104 --> 00:49:53.555
But my . . . The main focus is my mom and

555
00:49:53.555 --> 00:49:59.006
<c.p>my dad, and taking care of myself, my family.

556
00:49:59.006 --> 00:50:04.457
And that’s what I do and I enjoy it.

557
00:50:04.457 --> 00:50:06.880
I don’t get bored.

558
00:50:06.880 --> 00:50:09.303
I enjoy my life.

559
00:50:09.303 --> 00:50:22.628
They talking about operation on my left hand because I’m . . . They say I have arthritis but then . . . Alyssa just changed her, Daddy.

560
00:50:22.628 --> 00:50:25.050
Alyssa just changed her.

561
00:50:25.050 --> 00:50:29.896
But I don’t know nothings about the downfall.

562
00:50:29.896 --> 00:50:30.502
Yeah.

563
00:50:30.502 --> 00:50:37.164
But I’m glad y’all came because I love talking about Mossville.

564
00:50:37.164 --> 00:50:38.375
It’s gone.

565
00:50:38.375 --> 00:50:45.644
We don’t live there anymore, but it was all my good days.

566
00:50:45.644 --> 00:50:59.574
There was days where I don’t think they’re going to have no more days like that no more because it was fun times.

567
00:50:59.574 --> 00:51:03.814
The time that I spent in Mossville.

568
00:51:03.814 --> 00:51:14.110
But some people don’t want to leave Mossville, but now I pass through as I goes to [?].

569
00:51:14.110 --> 00:51:28.041
I just say I’m going to keep on doing what I’m doing and living in Moss Bluff making the best out of it.

570
00:51:28.041 --> 00:51:34.098
But a lot of people don’t know it’s a blessing.

571
00:51:34.098 --> 00:51:45.000
A lot of elderly people don’t know how much it’s a blessing for Sasol to buy Mossville.

572
00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:47.692
<v DRAGOON>Why do you think it’s a blessing?

573
00:51:47.692 --> 00:51:49.231
<v BERNARD>Why it’s a blessing?

574
00:51:49.231 --> 00:51:50.000
<v DRAGOON>[Agrees].

575
00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:56.655
<v BERNARD>Because they had all kind of . . . Even though I had fun there,

576
00:51:56.655 --> 00:52:07.191
they had like . . . They would go to the recreation and they would have meetings about the water being polluted.

577
00:52:07.191 --> 00:52:19.391
And my cousin getting sick, and I’m getting sick and I don’t know if it’s the water or that the mosquito, so.

578
00:52:19.391 --> 00:52:29.927
And I don’t look at nothing bad, but Mossville was a blessing and not a blessing in some areas.

579
00:52:29.927 --> 00:52:30.482
Yeah.

580
00:52:30.482 --> 00:52:42.127
It was close by the plant where a lot of people got hurt in the plant and they sued the plant.

581
00:52:42.127 --> 00:52:50.445
Got a little money for it, but still and all it made people sick, so.

582
00:52:50.445 --> 00:52:58.209
<v DRAGOON>You’ve seen a lot of people get sick from the pollution and the chemicals?

583
00:52:58.209 --> 00:52:59.318
<v BERNARD>Yes, ma’am.

584
00:52:59.318 --> 00:53:00.427
Yes, ma’am.

585
00:53:00.427 --> 00:53:10.409
And some got cancer and my daddy got cancer, but I don’t think it was from the plant.

586
00:53:10.409 --> 00:53:14.291
I forgot the cancer that he got,

587
00:53:14.291 --> 00:53:23.718
but a lot of people . . . Someone that I knew had babies who were playing in the dirt.

588
00:53:23.718 --> 00:53:25.936
It was two boys.

589
00:53:25.936 --> 00:53:31.482
One of the boys died because he was eating dirt.

590
00:53:31.482 --> 00:53:35.918
He would go outside and eat some dirt.

591
00:53:35.918 --> 00:53:43.682
So I don’t know if it was from the dirt or what it was,

592
00:53:43.682 --> 00:53:52.000
but a few people died in Mossville for unknown reason and that was scary.

593
00:53:52.000 --> 00:53:52.200
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember . . .?

594
00:53:52.000 --> 00:53:54.727
<v UNKNOWN>[. . .?]

595
00:53:54.727 --> 00:53:55.636
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

596
00:53:55.636 --> 00:54:00.182
Now what you say, babe?

597
00:54:00.182 --> 00:54:02.000
<v DRAGOON>[Pardon]?

598
00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:05.050
<v BERNARD>What you was going to say?

599
00:54:05.050 --> 00:54:10.642
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember who the . . . what the little boy’s name was?

600
00:54:10.642 --> 00:54:14.200
<v BERNARD>I just know he was a Braxton.

601
00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:14.708
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

602
00:54:14.708 --> 00:54:15.217
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

603
00:54:15.217 --> 00:54:21.825
I don’t know his full name I just know he was a Braxton.

604
00:54:21.825 --> 00:54:31.483
And they had quite a few other people, I don’t know their name, but they died and different things.

605
00:54:31.483 --> 00:54:41.650
And then my cousin, we call him No No, his name is Alvin Montgomery, he would go swimming and stuff.

606
00:54:41.650 --> 00:54:52.833
He got sick from . . . I think it was drugs that they put in his drink so I don’t know what it was,

607
00:54:52.833 --> 00:54:56.392
or the water I don’t know what.

608
00:54:56.392 --> 00:54:59.950
I don’t barely look at nothing negative.

609
00:54:59.950 --> 00:55:03.000
I just keep on living.

610
00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:05.919
<v DRAGOON>Were folks worried about the water?

611
00:55:05.919 --> 00:55:06.405
<v BERNARD>Yes.

612
00:55:06.405 --> 00:55:11.270
<v DRAGOON>Like did they talk about it growing up at all?

613
00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:11.757
<v BERNARD>Yes.

614
00:55:11.757 --> 00:55:22.459
They would . . . We would have to go pick up bottled water from the recreation center because we knew that water wasn’t good.

615
00:55:22.459 --> 00:55:29.270
And we would go pick up water from the rec and would have boxes,

616
00:55:29.270 --> 00:55:38.027
and boxes of water because I would bathe in it, in the water, but I wouldn’t drink it.

617
00:55:38.027 --> 00:55:39.000
Yeah.

618
00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:42.708
<v DRAGOON>And did people talk about why?

619
00:55:42.708 --> 00:55:47.653
Or was it something that everyone just knew?

620
00:55:47.653 --> 00:55:51.361
<v BERNARD>No, they had meetings and stuff.

621
00:55:51.361 --> 00:55:57.542
They been having meetings since I was a little girl.

622
00:55:57.542 --> 00:56:04.340
They would have meetings at the rec about the water pollution.

623
00:56:04.340 --> 00:56:19.792
My son, my baby son, he got into water pollution because he say he wanted to find a cure for Mossville water that they had.

624
00:56:19.792 --> 00:56:24.736
And that’s when he started doing water treatment.

625
00:56:24.736 --> 00:56:37.097
But they would have meetings and stuff, but I wouldn’t pay too much mind to it, but I would go.

626
00:56:37.097 --> 00:56:37.715
Yeah.

627
00:56:37.715 --> 00:56:46.986
And the air and stuff, there was always something they was complaining about in Mossville.

628
00:56:46.986 --> 00:56:51.931
I didn’t really look at it too much.

629
00:56:51.931 --> 00:57:01.201
I just say well that might be what made me sick and I don’t know.

630
00:57:01.201 --> 00:57:05.528
I try not to focus on it.

631
00:57:05.528 --> 00:57:06.146
Yeah.

632
00:57:06.146 --> 00:57:08.000
<v DRAGOON>Let’s see . . .

633
00:57:08.000 --> 00:57:18.534
<v BERNARD>But I love that y’all came here because I love talking about Mossville even though it’s gone.

634
00:57:18.534 --> 00:57:22.252
I’m going to always remember it.

635
00:57:22.252 --> 00:57:25.969
That’s where my family was raised.

636
00:57:25.969 --> 00:57:36.503
And right now there are few still is in Mossville and they having a reunion in July.

637
00:57:36.503 --> 00:57:41.460
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember any healers in the area?

638
00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:45.178
People who did like natural medicine?

639
00:57:45.178 --> 00:57:57.571
<v BERNARD>No. My auntie, my daddy’s sister, she would tell me she’d say, “Charlotte, I want you to come with me.”

640
00:57:57.571 --> 00:58:00.049
I’d say, “What’s wrong?”

641
00:58:00.049 --> 00:58:10.583
She said, “I want you though, we going to go to this lady to pray for you.”

642
00:58:10.583 --> 00:58:14.301
And I would go and pray.

643
00:58:14.301 --> 00:58:25.454
They must be a healer because she would put some kind of string around my stomach and stuff.

644
00:58:25.454 --> 00:58:28.552
But I didn’t like them.

645
00:58:28.552 --> 00:58:36.607
I didn’t really believe that I should be going to nothing like that.

646
00:58:36.607 --> 00:58:42.804
That I only have one healer and that was God.

647
00:58:42.804 --> 00:58:49.000
So that was a no-no for me to go.

648
00:58:49.000 --> 00:58:54.685
<v DRAGOON>Did your family have any other kinds of home remedies that you would do?

649
00:58:54.685 --> 00:58:57.933
For colds if . . . When your siblings got sick?

650
00:58:57.933 --> 00:59:05.242
<v BERNARD>I know when I used to catch headaches that auntie that wanted to take me to a treater,

651
00:59:05.242 --> 00:59:10.927
she would . . . she went and pick a moss she knew, what leaf to get,

652
00:59:10.927 --> 00:59:17.018
she would tell my daddy she said, “Let’s go walking in the woods,” she said,

653
00:59:17.018 --> 00:59:25.139
“I’m going to pick some leaves that I think Charlotte going to . . . That’s going to help her with her headaches.”

654
00:59:25.139 --> 00:59:39.352
And she went in the woods and she pick up the whatever she needed and she placed them all in the bed and I would sleep in the bed with all these leaves under me.

655
00:59:39.352 --> 00:59:49.909
And when I wake up I’d be soaking wet and she had to change my clothes because all that fever had done went in that leaf . . .

656
00:59:49.909 --> 00:59:52.752
<v UNKNOWN>Do you work with them too, baby?

657
00:59:52.752 --> 00:59:53.158
<v DRAGOON>[Pardon]?

658
00:59:53.158 --> 00:59:56.000
<v UNKNOWN>Do you . . . She work with y’all?

659
00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:57.000
<v BERNARD>[Agrees].

660
00:59:57.000 --> 00:59:58.000
Yeah.

661
00:59:58.000 --> 00:59:59.000
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

662
00:59:59.000 --> 01:00:03.000
<v UNKNOWN>[You be lucky?]

663
01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:09.266
<v DRAGOON>Thank you. [Laughs] Were . . . so you saw a treater.

664
01:00:09.266 --> 01:00:14.835
Did you see them more than one time?

665
01:00:14.835 --> 01:00:33.633
<v BERNARD>Most of the time I went to the treaters but they told me that I’m going to get better and then I didn’t really believe in treaters.

666
01:00:33.633 --> 01:00:34.329
Yeah.

667
01:00:34.329 --> 01:00:52.430
We folk . . . My mom was a well-known Catholic lady and we just went to church and believe in God and that’s what my dad did, too.

668
01:00:52.430 --> 01:00:58.000
<v DRAGOON>What will you miss most about Mossville?

669
01:00:58.000 --> 01:01:04.050
<v BERNARD>You can never get your childhood back once you leave it.

670
01:01:04.050 --> 01:01:15.600
And it was just a good time for my [. . .?] being brought up all together with not only my sisters and brothers,

671
01:01:15.600 --> 01:01:17.250
but my neighbors.

672
01:01:17.250 --> 01:01:21.100
We all stuck together and did things.

673
01:01:21.100 --> 01:01:25.500
Can’t get that back, but I’m moving on.

674
01:01:25.500 --> 01:01:29.900
<v DRAGOON>So we’re at about an hour right now.

675
01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:37.050
Is there anything you’d like to add as we wrap up our interview?

676
01:01:37.050 --> 01:01:48.050
<v BERNARD>No, but I’m just have dreams and vision of me walking again and I left Mossville . . . I went to Mossville.

677
01:01:48.050 --> 01:01:56.850
I was walking and when I go back, I’m going to go back before I walk.

678
01:01:56.850 --> 01:02:08.950
But I’m just going to stick to the idea that I’m going to be there once again walking and riding them horses.

679
01:02:08.950 --> 01:02:13.350
And I’m going to go to somebody house.

680
01:02:13.350 --> 01:02:16.650
Somebody going to have a horse.

681
01:02:16.650 --> 01:02:27.650
I’m going to ride through Mossville with my head in the air just running and hollering and riding that horse.

682
01:02:27.650 --> 01:02:31.500
One day I’m going to do that.

683
01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:35.900
I thank God for where I’m at now.

684
01:02:35.900 --> 01:02:37.000
<c.p>Yeah.

685
01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:45.444
<v DRAGOON>Well thank you so much for having us over to your home and telling us your story.

686
01:02:45.444 --> 01:02:45.940
<v BERNARD>[Agrees].

687
01:02:45.940 --> 01:02:47.927
I enjoyed telling it.

688
01:02:47.927 --> 01:02:48.921
<v DRAGOON>Thank you.

689
01:02:48.921 --> 01:02:55.377
<v BERNARD>When y’all told me that y’all was going to come have an interview,

690
01:02:55.377 --> 01:03:04.815
I said that I’m sure going to welcome y’all in because I love to tell a story about Mossville.

691
01:03:04.815 --> 01:03:07.298
And y’all that’s so funny.

692
01:03:07.298 --> 01:03:11.768
My mama have Alzheimer’s and she never watch TV.

693
01:03:11.768 --> 01:03:16.735
So when she came here, we have a big TV.

694
01:03:16.735 --> 01:03:21.205
Now she think those people on TV are real.

695
01:03:21.205 --> 01:03:28.159
She talks to them and tell them who she is and where she from.

696
01:03:28.159 --> 01:03:31.139
She say, “I have nine children.

697
01:03:31.139 --> 01:03:33.126
We live in Mossville.”

698
01:03:33.126 --> 01:03:37.596
So she still think she’s in Mossville at times.

699
01:03:37.596 --> 01:03:41.073
But we love having mama over here.

700
01:03:41.073 --> 01:03:47.033
Right now should be time for me to give her her medicine.

701
01:03:47.033 --> 01:03:47.530
<v DRAGOON>Alright.

702
01:03:47.530 --> 01:03:49.517
Let’s wrap it up.

703
01:03:49.517 --> 01:03:52.000
<v UNKNOWN>Thank you so much.

704
01:03:52.000 --> 01:03:52.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4621.

705
01:03:52.429 --> 01:03:52.857
End Session I.]
